      content:
          '**Upper Motor Neuron (Reflexic) Bowel** (injury above conus medullaris, typically T12 and above):\n- Intact sacral reflex arc (S2-S4)\n- **Increased** anal sphincter tone\n- Bulbocavernosus reflex **present**\n- Stool is retained due to spastic external anal sphincter\n- Management: **Digital stimulation** triggers reflex peristalsis and relaxation of the internal anal sphincter; chemical stimulants (bisacodyl/glycerin suppositories) augment reflex evacuation\n- This patient has UMN bowel (C7 injury, intact reflexes, increased tone)\n\n**Lower Motor Neuron (Areflexic) Bowel** (injury at or below conus medullaris/cauda equina):\n- Damaged sacral reflex arc\n- **Decreased/absent** anal sphincter tone (patulous anus)\n- Bulbocavernosus reflex **absent**\n- Risk of continuous stool leakage due to lax sphincter\n- Management: **Manual evacuation** (digital disimpaction) is the primary technique; stimulant suppositories are INEFFECTIVE (no reflex arc); high-fiber diet to form bulky stool; scheduled programs more frequently\n\n**Why This Matters**: Using the wrong bowel program is a common cause of failure. Giving digital stimulation to an LMN bowel patient is futile (no reflex to trigger). Conversely, relying on manual evacuation alone for a UMN bowel patient ignores the powerful reflex mechanism.\n\n**Workup for Constipation Complications**:\n- **Abdominal X-ray (KUB)**: Assess stool burden, rule out ileus or obstruction\n- **Basic metabolic panel**: Evaluate electrolytes, calcium (hypercalcemia can cause constipation), renal function\n- **Thyroid function**: Hypothyroidism is common and can worsen constipation\n- **Medication review**: Oxybutynin (anticholinergic) significantly slows bowel motility; gabapentin can also contribute\n- **If refractory**: Colonic transit study (Sitzmarker study) to assess motility; consider anorectal manometry',
